- 2026-08-25 session=this-session rule=shared-working-directory-concurrent-checkout what="govcon-factory issue #80 build: worked ~45min in a dedicated `git worktree add` (not the shared checkout, per this exact rule) on branch work/any-rfp-deliverable-issue-80, but never committed incrementally (implementation + live-network testing phase, was going to commit once proof runs completed). Mid-run, the worktree directory and its .git/worktrees/ admin metadata both vanished (confirmed: `git worktree list` no longer shows it, `ls` on the path 'No such file or directory', .git/worktrees/ itself gone) while a child process (live pipeline run) still had it open as cwd. The branch ref survived but at 0 commits (SHA identical to origin/main — never diverged), so nothing was recoverable from git itself. Root cause not confirmed (candidates: this machine's 6-hourly worktree-hygiene/prune automation misjudging a same-SHA-as-main branch as safe to remove despite dirty content, since D-026's fix only guards the 'ancestor of main' case, not the 'identical to main, zero commits' case; or another concurrent session's action) — flagged to Mike rather than guessed at. Lesson applied going forward: commit early/often even mid-implementation when working in a shared multi-session environment, not just 'use a worktree' — a worktree alone didn't protect against this machine's own automation or another session's action on it. All lost work was reproducible from this session's own context and was rebuilt with per-file commits this time."
2026-08-26 session=claude-sonnet5-goal-driver(this-session) rule=claim-discipline-before-commit what="filed and self-assigned issue #234 ('process proposals 0013-0018') and implemented all six, without checking whether another concurrent session had already claimed one of them individually. My initial duplicate-check search (gh issue list --search 'proposal-0013 OR proposal-0014 OR ... rubric-improve disposition') missed issue #240 ('rubric-improve: accept PROPOSAL-0014') because the title doesn't contain the literal search terms I used (no 'disposition', number format differs) - a full-text search with the wrong exact terms is not a substitute for reading the issue list itself, which I had already fetched minutes earlier and should have re-scanned instead of re-querying with guessed keywords. Result: PR #251 (this session) and PR #249 (other session, issue #240) both implement sop/CURRENT-PLAN + the same 4 pointer-file edits + the same new hook script for PROPOSAL-0014, independently, in parallel, both still open. Caught before either merged - fixing by rebasing #251 to drop the 0014-specific implementation and defer to #249 instead of shipping a duplicate."
2026-08-26 session=claude-fable-goal-driver rule=inert-regression-test what="four 'regression tests' across govcon-factory PRs #191/#206/#214/#215 passed with the production fix fully reverted - i.e. pinned nothing. Causes seen: asserting on inspect.getsource() text that also matched an explanatory comment; grepping a file for a helper name that survived on the import line; a fixture whose two UEIs differed by one character so the axis under test never fired and a name-match satisfied it alone; asserting against a whole document so a different source's similarly-worded line satisfied it. Three of the four were mine. Fix adopted: before claiming a test pins a fix, revert the production change in a scratch copy (git archive, never cp -R a worktree) and confirm the test fails; assert on VALUES (a module constant, the outgoing request payload, the rendered line) rather than source text or file contents, and test at the end of the chain - a constant nobody sends is the same bug one layer up."
2026-08-26 session=claude-fable-goal-driver rule=merge-on-review-of-a-different-commit what="govcon-factory PRs #191 and #198 were merged at stale heads (259ceb64, 08c1b00d) while the commits fixing their own reviewer-bot findings (a7fd02b, b91aba0) already existed on the branches - so both issues read as closed-and-fixed while main still shipped the defects (Mozilla UA in gates/gate_runner.py; the unpinned UEI identity mapping). Recovered via PRs #205/#206. Same root cause produced a CHANGES_REQUESTED and an APPROVED on the IDENTICAL sha (7d454e80) three minutes apart on #191, and 4-5 second approve-then-merge gaps on #177/#180. Filed as govcon-factory#183 (needs-mike). Fix recommended: a Tier-1 merge requires the approving review's commit_id to equal the head SHA being merged, and no CHANGES_REQUESTED standing on that same SHA; a soak timer alone does not catch it, since an OLD approval satisfies a soak trivially."
2026-08-26 session=claude-sonnet5-goal-driver(this-session) rule=verification-law.md what="a review subagent I spawned for govcon-factory PR #265 filed APPROVE citing '527/527 pass' from a local worktree/clone run, but the actual required CI check (self-hosted runner) was failing on that same commit - 4 new tests erroring on 'git commit --allow-empty' exit 128, likely missing user.name/user.email in a CI-fresh git config that the reviewer's local environment already had ambient. Caught before merging by checking gh pr checks myself instead of trusting the review's local-pass claim as sufficient. Lesson for future review-agent prompts in this repo: instruct them to check gh pr checks / actual CI status explicitly, not just run tests locally - a local pass is not the gate this repo's own SDLC defines, CI is."
2026-08-26 session=claude-sonnet5-goal-driver(this-session) rule=claim-discipline-before-commit what="third duplicate-work collision this session, this time on my OWN claimed issue (#268, DSBS/VetCert certified-pool wiring): merged PR #282 (factory.sbs_store.certified_ueis_within_cert_window + match.py:_apply_certified_pool_filter, simple UEI-set based) at 2026-08-26T03:46:45Z. A different concurrent session opened PR #288 seventeen seconds later (03:47:02Z) implementing the same D-031 join point independently as a richer factory.sbs_pool.query_certified_pool module (bonding-level fields, cert_window_months default 12 matching SOP G3, full firm records, honest-empty ok:false pattern) - discovered only because they posted an interface note on issue #267 (matcher rebuild) for coordination, not because any claim-lock caught it (I held the atomic claims/268.json lock the whole time; they apparently built without claiming, or the claim check didn't surface theirs to me). PR #288 touches the exact files (factory/sbs_store.py, factory/stages/match.py) my already-merged PR #282 touched, so it will conflict or silently duplicate the mechanism on rebase. Their interface is more complete than mine. Flagged on #267/PR #288 immediately; did not attempt to unilaterally close/override their PR since it merged after mine and has real additional value (bonding fields) mine lacks - reconciliation is a real design decision (whose interface wins), not a mechanical dedup, escalating for a joint call rather than deciding alone. Lesson: an atomic issue-claim lock does not prevent a DIFFERENT session from building the same capability under a DIFFERENT issue/PR number entirely - the lock only protects against two sessions claiming the SAME issue number, not against two sessions independently arriving at the same code-level join point from different angles.
2026-08-26 session=claude-fable-goal-driver rule=inert-regression-test what="fifth instance, and the sharpest: nine tests in govcon-factory tests/test_delivery_note_s789.py asserted the bare substring 'unknown, not clean' in note. Another source's block (S5) satisfies that on its own, so each assertion passed whether or not the source it was NAMED for said anything - a reviewer proved the S7 verdict could be deleted entirely on plain origin/main with 660 tests green. Fixed by scoping each assertion to the source's own bullet lines. Full set of five shapes seen this session: (1) inspect.getsource() matching an explanatory comment rather than the code; (2) a file-grep for a helper name satisfied by the import line; (3) a fixture whose two UEIs differed by one character so the axis under test never fired; (4) a whole-document assertion satisfied by a different source's similarly-worded line; (5) this one. Rule extracted: assert against the narrowest thing that can be true ONLY if the behavior is present - a module constant, the outgoing payload, the specific rendered line - never a whole file or a whole document."
2026-08-26 session=claude-fable-goal-driver rule=inert-regression-test what="sixth and worst instance, a new sub-shape: in govcon-factory PR #323 I wrote a leak-detection guard whose anti-vacuity test listed the string \"only if N>0 in data/\" as an example of a REAL HISTORICAL LEAK - and never grepped the codebase for it. It was live, reaching 9/9 real Opportunity Packets and 2/2 RFP Response Starters, found only because a reviewer rendered every run in runs/ and scanned the output with my own pattern. This is worse than a test that pins nothing: it is a test that documents a live defect and calls it history, while its passing status implies the class is handled. Rule extracted: when writing a detector, grep the codebase for every example string you put in its test - an example is a claim that the case is closed, and a detector shipped alongside a live instance of what it detects is a false assurance, not a partial win."
2026-08-26 session=claude-fable-goal-driver rule=inert-regression-test what="three MORE variants inside a single PR (govcon-factory #323), all found by mutation review after I had already adopted mutation testing as my standard. Every product fix was correct; three could be reverted with the suite green. (a) a test that could not reach the code it was written for - it hand-built the intermediate dict, so it never called the function containing the leak; (b) a correctness fix with no assertion at all, because the only test covering that file scanned for text PATTERNS and both the right and wrong strings are pattern-clean; (c) I tested the HELPER instead of the CALL SITE, so it passed with the call site reverted to bypassing the helper. Also caught: my fixture used the literal string 'None' where the assertion looks for a bare Python None in rendered output - the fixture would have masked the exact thing it asserted. Rules extracted: assert where the value REACHES THE READER, not where it is produced; build test inputs through real production code paths rather than hand-constructing intermediates; and check that fixture values cannot themselves satisfy the assertion."
2026-08-26 session=claude-fable-goal-driver rule=inert-regression-test what="tenth instance, new shape: a fixture that STARTS DOWNSTREAM OF THE BUG. In govcon-factory PR #323 my hygiene tests built the aggregates dict directly and rendered from it, so they could never see a defect in the notice->aggregates code above them. A reviewer found the consequence live: .get(key, default) does not fire when a key is PRESENT AND NONE, and real SAM notices carry set_aside present-but-None on 13/18 runs, so the client-facing Market Snapshot rendered '**Set-aside as posted:** None' on 2/2 real runs. When I added one test that starts from a NOTICE and runs through _compute_snapshot_aggregates, it immediately found two MORE instances of the same shape (Response Deadline, Timeline Pressure). Rules extracted: build the fixture at the TOP of the chain the defect can occur in, not at the boundary of the function under test - a downstream fixture cannot see an upstream bug no matter how good the assertion; and treat .get(k, default) as a smell wherever the source may emit an explicit None, since the default silently never fires."
2026-08-26 session=claude-fable-goal-driver rule=inert-regression-test what="eleventh shape, and the sharpest demonstration yet: a hand-built fixture does not only miss UPSTREAM bugs, it silently ROTS against upstream SHAPE changes. In govcon-factory PR #323 my _snapshot() fixture hand-built the aggregates dict; main then added two required keys (is_sdvosb, competition_rollup.sole_source_share) and 7 of my 14 tests errored on merge. The single test that survived was the one built through _compute_snapshot_aggregates - the real production path - because it picked the new keys up for free. Same file, same session, its own argument landing on its own fixture. Separately in the same review: I claimed a code branch was 'unreachable until issue #334 lands' without checking - #334 was ALREADY MERGED, so the branch was live, its sentinel-keyed condition was dead, and the WRONG sentence was rendering. Rules: build fixtures through the real production entry point so they track shape changes automatically; and never assert 'unreachable until X lands' without checking X's actual state - reachability claims are verifiable in one command."
2026-08-26 session=claude-fable-goal-driver rule=claimed-fix-not-in-tree what="THIRD occurrence tonight of the same mechanical failure: a python string-replace did not match, silently made no change, and I reported the fix as done without re-reading the file. Instances: (1) the k.arg AST-guard fix on govcon-factory PR #266 - caught only because I re-ran the reviewer's mutation instead of trusting my own edit; (2) an earlier replace in the same PR; (3) PR #323, where I claimed BARE_PY_VALUE had been widened to drop its colon anchor - it never was, and the PR BODY shipped the false claim until a reviewer grepped the file. Root cause is not carelessness: str.replace() and sed return success whether or not they matched, so a no-op edit is indistinguishable from a successful one unless you look. Rule: after any programmatic edit, grep for the CHANGED TEXT before claiming the change - one command. Corollary, which is the more general lesson from this session: 'I wrote a fix' and 'the fix is in the tree' are different facts, and only the second one is worth reporting."
2026-08-26 session=claude-sonnet5-goal-driver(this-session) rule=shared-working-directory-concurrent-checkout what="nearly dispatched a subagent to build 'draft generation' for govcon-factory issue #293, based on inspecting factory/outreach.py in the shared ~/govcon-factory checkout - which was silently on a stale branch (work/single-queue-issue-243, another session's leftover checkout state) rather than origin/main, and its render_touch() looked like it had zero merge-field interpolation. Checked git show origin/main:factory/outreach.py before actually dispatching and found the real current main already has full interpolation (_RenderFields, per-field shortening helpers, [[MISSING:key]] markers) via already-merged PR #356/issue #352 - would have been a pure duplicate of already-shipped work. Caught before any subagent was spawned; posted a correction to issue #293. Lesson: when the shared checkout is known-dirty/on-a-different-branch (as flagged multiple times this session by other agents), never read its working-tree file contents as a proxy for 'current main' - always git show origin/main:<path> or a fresh worktree, even for a quick read-only sanity check, not just before committing."
2026-08-26 session=claude-fable-goal-driver rule=never-measured-reported-as-measured what="FOURTH instance, and I introduced it while fixing the third. Fixing bare 'None' renders in govcon-factory client documents (#366), I replaced them with 'Not stated in the notice' - but agency/sub_tier go None on a best-effort org-lookup MISS (_fetch_agency_name's own docstring), not because the notice omitted them. So the deliverable rendered '**Notice:** Not stated in the notice — Untitled notice': fluent, confident, and false about a document the pipeline never read. The reviewer's judgment is the durable part: a confident false statement is WORSE in a client deliverable than a self-reporting 'None', because None is visibly broken and prose is not. My own test could not distinguish honest from false - it only checked the string wasn't 'N/A'/'None'. Rules: when replacing a null with prose, the prose must describe what the SYSTEM did (not retrieved / not screened / not pulled), never what the SOURCE contains, unless the system actually read the source and found it absent; and a test over placeholder text must assert the CLAIM is true, not merely that the placeholder is not the literal 'None'."
